APS-015 — Policy Versioning

Document ID: APS-015
Title: Policy Versioning
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Versioning Model (CPVM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Versioning Model establishes the principles, structures, governance, compatibility rules, and lifecycle governing the evolution of Canonical Policy Objects while preserving identity, traceability, reproducibility, and engineering integrity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Policy version architecture
Version identification
Semantic versioning
Compatibility
Policy lineage
Rollback
Migration
Version governance

This specification applies to every Canonical Policy Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Versioning is the governed process of evolving a Canonical Policy Object through successive immutable versions while preserving policy identity, governance, compatibility, and historical continuity.

Versioning evolves policy behavior.

It SHALL NOT alter historical policy versions.

4. Objectives

The Policy Versioning Model SHALL:

Preserve policy identity.
Support controlled evolution.
Enable rollback.
Preserve reproducibility.
Support coexistence.
Preserve governance.
Support interoperability.
Maintain traceability.
5. Versioning Principles

Every Canonical Policy Object SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Persistent Identity

The Policy ID SHALL remain constant across all versions.

Immutable Versions

Approved policy versions SHALL NOT be modified.

Explicit Evolution

Every policy change SHALL produce a new version.

Version Traceability

Every version SHALL preserve complete lineage.

Compatibility Awareness

Version compatibility SHALL be explicitly declared.

Governance

Every version SHALL undergo governed approval.

6. Canonical Version Structure

Every policy version SHALL define:

Property	Description
Policy ID	Persistent policy identity
Version ID	Unique version identifier
Version Number	Canonical semantic version
Parent Version	Previous version
Release Identifier	Associated policy release
Compatibility Class	Compatibility declaration
Effective Period	Validity interval
Status	Lifecycle state
7. Semantic Versioning

Atlas adopts semantic versioning for policies.

Major.Minor.Patch
Major

Represents incompatible policy behavior changes.

Example:

3.0.0

↓

4.0.0
Minor

Represents compatible functional extensions.

Example:

4.2.0

↓

4.3.0
Patch

Represents corrections that preserve policy semantics.

Example:

4.3.2

↓

4.3.3
8. Compatibility Classes

Atlas defines four canonical compatibility classes.

Class	Description
Fully Compatible	No operational changes required
Backward Compatible	Existing consumers continue functioning
Forward Compatible	Supports future policy consumers
Breaking Change	Migration required

Compatibility SHALL be explicitly declared.

9. Version Coexistence

Multiple policy versions MAY coexist.

Example:

Security Policy

v3

↓

v4

↓

v5

Project A → v3

Project B → v4

Project C → v5

Each evaluation SHALL explicitly reference the policy version used.

10. Migration Model

Every breaking policy version SHOULD provide migration guidance.

Migration guidance MAY define:

Behavioral differences
Required configuration updates
Compatibility impacts
Deprecated features
Recommended migration path

Migration guidance SHALL itself be versioned.

11. Rollback

Rollback SHALL define:

Target version
Rollback justification
Approval authority
Effective timestamp
Impact assessment
Recovery plan

Rollback SHALL create a new governance event.

Historical versions SHALL remain unchanged.

12. Policy Lineage

Every policy version SHALL preserve lineage.

Policy

↓

v1

↓

v2

↓

v3

↓

v4

Branches SHALL preserve parent-child relationships.

13. Version Governance

Every policy version SHALL define:

Author
Reviewer
Approver
Release authority
Change summary
Applicable governance domain
Audit references

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the version lifecycle.

14. Version Quality

Every policy version SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Consistency
Explainability
Traceability
Compatibility
Governance compliance
Reproducibility
Auditability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

15. Version Lifecycle

Every policy version SHALL support:

Draft

↓

Review

↓

Approved

↓

Released

↓

Active

↓

Superseded

↓

Archived

Lifecycle SHALL remain independently traceable.

16. Conformance

A Policy Versioning implementation conforms if it:

Preserves policy identity.
Creates immutable versions.
Implements semantic versioning.
Declares compatibility.
Supports rollback.
Maintains policy lineage.
Preserves governance metadata.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted compatibility analysis.
Automated migration planning.
Distributed policy repositories.
Federated version synchronization.
Temporal policy queries.
Predictive policy evolution.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical versioning semantics.

